WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.140
Heap memory safety depends on correct size
tracking.

2
00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:06.563
A heap pointer does not automatically
include the allocation size,

3
00:00:06.643 --> 00:00:09.987
the number of initialized elements, or the
current logical length.

4
00:00:10.067 --> 00:00:12.999
That metadata must be stored and
maintained by the program.

5
00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:15.660
Byte counts and element counts are
different units.

6
00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:19.846
Allocating space for elements requires
multiplying the count by the element size,

7
00:00:19.926 --> 00:00:22.742
and that calculation must be checked
before allocation.

8
00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:26.752
Copying, indexing, formatting, and parsing
should use the correct unit for the

9
00:00:26.832 --> 00:00:27.418
operation.

10
00:00:27.498 --> 00:00:31.604
Integer overflow in allocation-size
calculations can break safety assumptions.

11
00:00:31.684 --> 00:00:35.732
A wrapped size can request less memory
than the program later treats as available.

12
00:00:35.812 --> 00:00:39.038
Defensive code validates counts,
additions, multiplications,

13
00:00:39.118 --> 00:00:41.933
and conversions before memory is allocated
or accessed.

14
00:00:42.013 --> 00:00:44.067
Current length and capacity also differ.

15
00:00:44.147 --> 00:00:47.021
Capacity is how much space the allocation
can safely hold.

16
00:00:47.101 --> 00:00:50.503
Length is how much of that space currently
contains meaningful data.

17
00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:52.577
Operations such as append, parse, read,

18
00:00:52.657 --> 00:00:55.179
and resize must update these values
consistently.

19
00:00:55.259 --> 00:00:58.603
Object layout matters when a heap object
contains headers, arrays,

20
00:00:58.683 --> 00:01:01.088
flexible trailing data, or nested
allocations.

21
00:01:01.168 --> 00:01:05.274
Reviewers should check that every access
stays within the object boundary that was

22
00:01:05.354 --> 00:01:08.580
actually allocated, not the boundary the
code hoped to allocate.
